  "Question": "If \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x^4+x^2}{x+1},\\) then \\(f(i),\\) where \\(i=\\sqrt{-1},\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1+i\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(-1\\)",
    "D": "\\(0\\)",
    "E": "\\(-1-i\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The defining identity \\(i^2=-1\\) gives \\(i^4=(i^2)^2=1\\). Substituting these values makes the numerator vanish:\n\\[\nf(i)=\\frac{i^4+i^2}{i+1}\n=\\frac{1-1}{1+i}=0.\n\\]\nThe denominator \\(1+i\\) is nonzero because its imaginary part is \\(1\\). Therefore \\(\\boxed{f(i)=0}\\), choice D.",
